# Experimental guidelines

Every rule below is here because breaking it already produced a wrong number in
this repository, and the wrong number was published before anyone noticed. None
of them are hypothetical. The incident is cited so the rule can be argued with
rather than obeyed blindly.

The habit these add up to: a measurement is a claim about data the system has
never been fitted to, produced by a metric that can tell good from bad, over the
universe the ground truth can actually speak about. Anything less is a
description of the fit, not a result.

## 1. Never score on data that training touched

Training here means anything that consumed the labels: gradient steps, GEPA
rollouts, prompt selection, threshold tuning, or a human reading the errors and
editing a signature. Selection is training. A prompt chosen because it scored
best on a split is fitted to that split.

- Score on the holdout split and nothing else. `sample_random_splits` reserves
  one for exactly this purpose.
- State the split next to every number. "F1 0.9906" is not a result;
  "F1 0.9906 on holdout" is.
- If you must report a validation number, label it as one in the same sentence.

**The incident.** `serf benchmark --sample-records 1000 --seed 42` drew
_precisely_ GEPA's validation split. `sample_records` and
`sample_random_splits` both shuffle the same entity list with
`random.Random(seed)` and walk it pulling whole match groups, and splits fill
validation first. Measured overlap was 100% on all five datasets and 0% with
train. Every trained-versus-untrained comparison taken that way reports the
score GEPA selected the prompt by. `training_overlap` now computes this share
and `serf benchmark` warns when it is nonzero — treat that warning as a failed
run, not a note.

**How it is enforced.** `serf benchmark` scores the holdout split by default,
drawing the same partition from the same budgets and seed that `serf train`
uses, so the records it scores are the ones training reserved. Naming a split
training read is refused outright rather than warned about, unless
`benchmarks.require_disjoint_eval` is turned off to measure the fit on purpose.
`serf train` ends by scoring both prompts on the holdout, and that comparison
decides whether it reports an improvement.

Figures published before this landed are validation scores, including the
0.9906-versus-0.9737 DBLP-ACM comparison, and are labelled as such wherever
they appear.

## 2. Two independent draws need two independent seeds

Reusing one seed across two sampling functions does not give you two samples. It
gives you correlated samples, and if the functions walk the same list the same
way, it gives you the same sample. That is the whole mechanism of the incident
above: `optimize.seed: 42` and `--seed 42` were never meant to coincide.

Derive per-purpose seeds (`seed + offset`, or a named namespace) rather than
threading one integer everywhere, and verify disjointness by id rather than
assuming it.

## 3. Reserve the evaluation budget before the training budget

Fill validation and holdout first, then give training whatever is left.
Training data is the thing you have too much of; evaluation data is the thing
that silently rounds to nothing. `sample_random_splits` fills val, then
holdout, then train for this reason.

**The incident.** The older `sample_blocked_splits` took train blocks first, up
to `train_blocks=1000`, and drew val and holdout from whatever was left.
Blocking DBLP-ACM's 4,910 records produced only 82 blocks averaging ~60
records, covering nearly the whole table, so train took 81 blocks / 4,909
records and left **1 record for val and 0 for holdout**. Neither budget was
honored and nothing failed. See rule 4 for what that did.

**How it is enforced.** Writing this rule surfaced that fill order alone was
not enough: `_scaled_budgets` used to scale all three budgets _proportionally_
when they exceeded the dataset, so a dominant train budget still starved
evaluation. `_scaled_budgets(4910, 200, 200, 10**9)` returned `(0, 0, 4909)` —
the same 1-record-validation failure through a different door. It is now
`_fit_budgets`, which pays each budget in full before the next one gets
anything, in the order val, holdout, train. The shortfall always lands on
train, because train is the split that can absorb it.

## 4. Prove the metric can discriminate before trusting a run

Before reading any result, confirm the metric separates a known-good
configuration from a known-bad one on the data you are about to use. A metric
that returns the same value for everything does not fail loudly; it returns a
number and the run exits 0.

- Check that the split contains scoreable positives at all.
  `sample_random_splits` logs a warning when a split holds no gold pairs.
- Check that at least two candidates got different scores. Identical scores mean
  the selection was arbitrary.

**The incident.** The starved val split from rule 3 was packed into synthetic
blocks of _random_ leftover records, which contain essentially no true duplicate
pairs, so pair-level F1 read 0.0 no matter how good the prompt was. GEPA
proposed 9 candidates, all scored exactly 0.0, and it returned candidate 0 —
the _unoptimized_ instructions — as the winner. The run reported success and
the result was nearly published as an optimized-versus-baseline comparison.

## 5. Score over the universe the ground truth can state

A gold standard is defined over a restricted set of claims. Scoring a
prediction that falls outside that set counts the metric's blind spot as a model
error.

**The incident.** SERF's benchmark gold is bipartite: pairs are
`(a_id, b_id + RIGHT_ID_OFFSET)`, so it can never assert that two left records
or two right records match. `evaluate_resolution` computed
`fp = len(pred) - tp` with no source filter, making every same-source pair an
automatic false positive. On DBLP-Scholar all **783** false positives were
same-source and **zero** were genuine cross-source errors. That produced a
published 0.4846 F1 "collapse" from transitive closure that never happened. The
correct move was to score only the cross-source projection, not to stop the
matcher from merging same-source records — those merges were right.

## 6. Score the system's output, not its log of intermediate assertions

Convert predictions into the structure the system actually claims, then score
that. Scoring the raw intermediate artifact makes the result depend on
bookkeeping the model had no opinion about.

**The incident.** A matcher emitting pairs is asserting a partition: a-b plus
b-c says a, b and c are one entity. `collect_pairs` flattened each resolved
entity into master-to-source pairs only, so a group spanning 2 left and 2 right
records emitted 3 pairs while asserting 6 — dropping 2 genuine cross-source
pairs as false negatives. `connected_components` now recovers the partition
before scoring. Recovering it raised mean F1 from 0.8775 to 0.9390 with no
change to the matcher.

## 7. Never compare numbers produced by different scorers

A scoring change invalidates every number measured before it. Re-measure both
sides or report neither.

**The incident.** A trained prompt read F1 0.9874 and an untrained one 0.9737,
and the gap was presented as the effect of training. The trained run predated
two scoring fixes by hours. Re-measured under the same scorer the trained run
read 0.9906. Before trusting any historical figure, compare the run's timestamp
against `git log` for the metric code — `git log -S` on the metric name finds
when a figure was produced.

## 8. Measure run-to-run variance before believing an effect

An effect smaller than the noise is not an effect. Get the noise floor by
rerunning one identical configuration, and report effects against it.

**The incident.** DBLP-ACM's one-iteration F1 read 0.9788 in one arm and 0.9693
in another on identical prompts and samples — about 0.01 of drift from LLM
nondeterminism alone. The iteration effects (0.05–0.10) clear that; the
per-dataset signature effect (+0.0135 mean) barely does, which is why it is
reported as a mean over five datasets rather than as a win on any one.

## 9. Subsample by sampling, never by slicing

Taking the first N of anything inherits whatever order the producer happened to
emit, and that order is rarely random. Blocking emits blocks by cluster, so
head-slicing drops a systematic rather than a representative part of a split.
Use `random.Random(seed).sample(...)` and log the seed. `_apply_cap` in
`serf.dspy.train` does this.

## 10. Do not shrink evaluation data for a cost you have not measured

Check what the cost actually scales with before trading measurement fidelity for
it.

**The incident.** `optimize.val_blocks` capped validation at 20 examples to save
budget. But dspy's `GEPA.auto_budget` derives its trial count from the candidate
count alone — `num_trials = max(2 * (num_preds * 2) * log2(num_candidates),
1.5 * num_candidates)` — and valset size enters only as linear full-evaluation
terms. At `auto=light` the cap saved 10% of rollouts — 460 against 512 — while
it discarded 13 of 33 validation
examples and coarsening Pareto selection to 5% granularity. Train is now
uncapped (dspy asks for "as large a trainset as possible", and each step draws
only `reflection_minibatch_size` from it regardless of size) and val sits at 35,
dspy's own threshold below which it already evaluates the full valset every
step.

## 11. Report the denominator you actually scored

If the metric filters, projects, or deduplicates predictions, publish both the
raw and the scored count. A reader comparing "predicted pairs" against
precision, unaware that two thirds were dropped as unscoreable, will draw a
conclusion neither number supports. `serf benchmark` prints Predicted Pairs and
Scored Pairs side by side because the gap between them is where rule 5 hid.

## 12. Decompose before explaining

When a result surprises you, split it before theorizing. Precision against
recall, false positives by category, errors by dataset. The 0.4846 "collapse"
survived because it was explained (closure amplification — plausible, wrong)
instead of decomposed. One query bucketing false positives by source pair ended
it.

## 13. Make long runs fail loudly

A long run that half-fails is worse than one that crashes, because it produces a
complete-looking result. Refresh credentials rather than minting one static
token, and treat scoreable-zero results as suspect until you have read the log.

**The incident.** A Vertex access token was minted once and passed to `dspy.LM`
as a static `api_key`. It expired about an hour into the run, and the last
**215** rollouts — roughly a quarter of the budget — were each scored as a
spurious zero and fed to GEPA as evidence. Separately, 257 responses were
silently truncated at `max_tokens` and discarded as unparseable.

## 14. Keep external comparisons like-for-like

Before putting a number beside a published one, confirm both measure the same
task. SERF runs end-to-end resolution — blocking, matching, merging, closure —
while most benchmark leaderboard entries score pair classification over
pre-supplied candidate pairs. Those are not the same number, and the difference
favors whichever system was handed its candidates. State the task alongside the
figure.

## 15. Every number needs a command, a seed, and an artifact

A result you cannot regenerate is an anecdote. Record the exact command, the
seed, the commit, and the path to the run log next to the figure — that is what
`experiments/*.md` is for. Contradicting a published table later requires
knowing precisely how it was produced; the fixes in rules 5, 6 and 7 were only
possible because the original runs were reproducible.
